  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "A Silver Alert is a public notification system used in the U.S. to locate missing seniors, particularly those with Alzheimer’s, dementia, or other cognitive impairments. Activated by law enforcement, it broadcasts information via media and emergency systems to find at-risk individuals, usually aged 60-65 or older. This video explains the purpose of a Silver Alert:31sNBC ConnecticutYouTube • May 6, 2025 31s Key Aspects of Silver Alerts Activation Criteria: Generally requires the missing person to have a diagnosed cognitive impairment, be over a certain age (typically 60 or 65, depending on state law), and be considered in danger. How it Works: Similar to AMBER Alerts, they use media outlets, highway signs, and emergency alerts to inform the public. Purpose: To quickly mobilize the public in locating vulnerable, missing adults. Criticisms/Limitations: There have been few independent evaluations of their real-world effectiveness. Additionally, some, including this YouTube video, point out that families may face stigma or blame when a loved one with dementia wanders, which can add to the emotional burden. What to DoIf you see a Silver Alert, note the vehicle, clothing, or location description. Do not approach the individual directly; instead, call 911 immediately with the details.",
